    "multirow_qpE4Em": {
      "type": "multirow",
      "blocks": {
        "row_fcbNVe": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/imgi_14_high-end-bedding-travel.jpg",
            "caption": "from the high seas",
            "heading": "Queen Asa was not the only Viking enraptured by eiderdown.",
            "text": "<p>In 870 AD, Ottar the Viking arrived at the palace of King Alfred the Great, in the Anglo-Saxon kingdom of Wessex. He captivated the royal court with colourful stories of his intrepid journey. As the fire blazed and the wine was poured, he explained the simple motive behind his quest: “I wanted to find out how far north my country ended.”</p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "button_link": ""
          }
        },
        "row_7cjy37": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "image": "shopify://shop_images/imgi_15_norwegian-history.jpg",
            "caption": "",
            "heading": "",
            "text": "<p>Ottar had set sail from Hålogaland, near the Arctic Circle, and voyaged the ‘North Way’ – known as ‘Norvegr’. During his travels he collected fur pelts, feathers and precious down. After many months, his voyage took him south along the Norwegian coast to Denmark, and finally to the court of King Alfred, where his treasures were eagerly received. Alfred was so impressed that he added Ottar’s stories to his version of the Compendious History of the World.</p>",
            "button_label": "",
            "button_link": ""
          }
        }
      },
